WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:06.914
"The TAME Trial—
Targeting Aging with Metformin"

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.280
Given the evidence

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.440
that diabetics
taking the drug metformin

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:11.760
may live longer

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.800
than people who
don't even have diabetes

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.320
suggests metformin
has an anti-aging effect,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.380
slowing the diseases of aging
and, in doing so,

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.900
extending both
health and lifespans,

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.820
but you don't know

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.220
until you put it to the test.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.400
There are a few small,
brief trials of metformin

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:30.900
on healthy volunteers,
for example,

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.300
randomizing
a dozen or so individuals

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.700
to metformin
or placebo for a few weeks,

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.820
comparing the effects
on muscle and fat biopsies.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.160
Beneficial changes
in gene expression

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.260
in various
aging pathways were noted,

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.780
along with objective
improvements in risk factors,

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.240
such as LDL cholesterol
and triglycerides,

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.060
but what we'd really like
are hard endpoints,

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:52.740
like disease outcomes and death.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.380
Enter the TAME trial,

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.620
Targeting Aging with Metformin.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.960
Spearheaded by the nonprofit

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.700
American Federation
for Aging Research,

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.220
TAME has the ambitious goal

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.640
of randomizing 3,000
nondiabetic individuals

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.380
aged 65-79

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.300
to metformin or placebo

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.680
over a period of six years

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.620
to assess the effect
on age-related conditions,

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:17.640
such as heart attacks,

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:18.480
stroke,

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:19.380
cancer,

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.520
dementia,

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:21.540
and death.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.260
Current treatments
"just exchange one disease

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.480
for another," railed
the principal investigator.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.920
Someone cured of cancer

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.320
may just drop dead the next year
from a heart attack.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.220
"What we want to show
is that if we delay aging,

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.340
that's the best way
to delay disease."

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.300
Sadly,

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.420
though the TAME trial
received FDA approval in 2015,

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.180
it has yet
to get off the ground.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.280
You can guess why.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.980
As the editor-in-chief
of an anti-aging journal put it,

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:53.460
"There is no way in hell
to make money out of it."

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.220
Metformin's patent
expired decades ago,

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.620
and generics retail
at pennies a pill.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.980
It's considered
a "genuinely good use

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.480
of 75 million dollars,"

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.160
but the researchers
have only evidently

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:06.780
been able to raise $11 million

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:07.980
from private donors.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.460
Some in Silicon Valley
tech circles

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:12.960
aren't waiting for the results.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.660
Apparently, some techies
have started taking metformin

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:16.920
in a bid to live longer,

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.260
though it's only officially
available by prescription

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:20.460
for diabetes.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.880
Metformin is cheap,
relatively safe,

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.100
and one of the most
promising drugs to combat

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.300
age-related diseases,

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.400
but there are reasons

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.320
to temper our expectations.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.680
Though it's been referred
to as a "magic drug,"

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.900
the longevity benefits in mice

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.700
were small and dose dependent.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.100
At best, it may increase
the average lifespan

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.720
of certain mice by five percent,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.260
but at a higher dose,
actually shortens lifespan.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.740
Much of the enthusiasm
for the TAME trial

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.080
rests on it acting
as a test case

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.200
to successfully get regulatory
authorities, like the FDA,

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.240
to formally recognize
any treatment

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.860
for the aging process itself,

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.560
in hopes that this will
arouse Big Pharma

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.260
to invest large-scale R&amp;D
to advance the field.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.920
Further reservations
about the panacean prospects

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.800
placed on metformin
arise from the landmark

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.140
Diabetes
Prevention Program study

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.000
published in the New England
Journal of Medicine.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.660
Thousands of prediabetic
individuals were randomized

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:20.760
to metformin
or placebo for years

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.580
to see if the drug
could prevent their prediabetes

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.920
from turning
into full-blown diabetes.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.120
It worked,

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.570
reducing overall
diabetes incidence by 31 percent

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:31.500
compared to placebo,

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.120
but it didn't for everyone.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.640
For example, it didn't appear
to significantly benefit

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.200
those 60 years or older

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.960
and only seemed
to help those most at risk--

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.880
those with class II obesity

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.320
and the highest blood sugars.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.720
Those with lower
(but still elevated)

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.240
blood sugars,
and those under a BMI of 35

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.580
did not have their diabetes risk

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.020
significantly dampened
by the drug.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.060
One small study even found that,

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.620
while metformin alleviated

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.600
the insulin resistance
of diabetics

00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.640
and those with
a family history of diabetes,

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:03.120
metformin actually worsened
the insulin resistance

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.460
(the cause of prediabetes
and type 2 diabetes)

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:07.920
of nondiabetic obese individuals

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.320
that didn't have
a genetic predisposition.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.440
If you have diabetes,
it can make things better;

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.440
if you don't,
it may make things worse.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.060
So, healthier individuals

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:20.220
may not reap the benefits
of metformin

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:21.660
that some try to extrapolate

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.360
from those
longevity studies on diabetics.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.460
In fact, even those researchers

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.800
are explicit that
they don't think metformin

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:29.820
is ready for prime time

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.500
as an anti-aging intervention

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:32.820
by the general population.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.660
What's safer
and works even better?

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.300
There was a third arm

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:41.520
of the Diabetes Prevention
Program study.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.680
Thousands of prediabetics
were randomized

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.000
to metformin or placebo

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.400
or lifestyle changes--

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.040
a cholesterol-lowering
low-fat diet and exercise,

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.540
at least about 20 minutes a day

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:54.120
moderate intensity exercise,
such as brisk walking.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.780
Compared to the placebo group,
those taking metformin

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:00.600
were 31 percent less likely
to develop diabetes.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:04.200
The lifestyle group
was 58 percent less likely.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.180
Making simple lifestyle changes

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:08.700
was nearly
twice as effective as metformin

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:10.620
(and with fewer side effects--

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.480
for example,
six times fewer cases

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.100
of gastrointestinal distress).

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.740
Other randomized
controlled trials

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:17.520
have found the same thing--

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.560
lifestyle approaches superior

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:22.080
to drug-based approaches
for diabetes prevention.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.040
And, that 60 or so percent
drop in risk was not among those

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:28.200
who actually made
the lifestyle change,

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.240
just those instructed to do so.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.880
Another diabetes
prevention trial

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.440
published in the New England
Journal of Medicine

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.600
that found the same
58 percent reduction

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.700
in diabetes risk
among those asked

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:39.720
to make lifestyle changes

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:41.580
drilled down to see
what the benefit was

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:43.440
for those who fully complied.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:44.760
Only a fraction

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.680
of the lifestyle
intervention group

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:48.360
met the modest goals.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:49.440
Only about a third

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.360
increased their exercise,
for example.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:52.740
Only about a quarter

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:55.500
met the minimum
recommended intake of fiber

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:57.240
(found concentrated
in whole plant foods)

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:59.220
or cut down
on enough saturated fat

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.740
(mostly dairy, dessert,
chicken, and pork).

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:05.100
But, they did better
than the control group,

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:08.340
and fewer of them developed
diabetes because of it.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:10.380
But, what if you look
just at the folks

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:11.580
who actually made all

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:13.080
(or even just 4 out of 5)

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:14.760
of the recommended
lifestyle changes?

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.760
They had zero diabetes--

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.340
none of them got diabetes
over the course of the study,

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:23.760
meaning effectively
a 100 percent drop in risk.

